{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjZx28Ebau1TnNWMvU8b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JhJWvuhoxmj5o1wgMhZsct","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with a double barline and \"pianiss.\" underneath. Its first bar has two grace notes, then Bb4 8th and A4 G4 16ths. That is the second half of bar 16: grace G4 A4, [pp] Bb4, A4, G4. The bar's opening (G4 F#4) must be at the end of the line before, so the line starts partway through bar 16. Bar 2: grace, F#4 8th, Eb4 D4 16ths, grace Bb4 C5, D5 8th, C5 Bb4 16ths. That is bar 17. Bar 3: grace, A4, G4 F#4, grace G4 A4, Bb4, A4 G4. That is bar 18. Bar 4: grace, F#4 8th, A4 F#4 D4 16ths (the triplet), G3 8th, G4 8th. That is bar 19. Bar 5: G4 quarter slurred to F#4 8th, then D4 8th. That is bar 20, with \"cresc.\" written below near here. Bar 6: B4 with a natural sign, A4 8th, D4 8th. That is bar 21. Bar 7: D5 quarter slurred to C5 8th, then D5 8th. That is bar 22. A barline closes the line. Nothing in the line contradicts the edition.","right":true,"insights":"The double barline at the start is misleading. The line actually begins with the second half of bar 16, and the \"pianiss.\" matching the edition's [pp] on Bb4 confirms this. The Bb/A/G figures with grace notes in bars 17–18 and the triplet with the G3–G4 leap in bar 19 make the bars easy to tell apart. A clear view of the end of the previous line, showing where bar 16 starts, would have settled the start at once."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5664,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5664},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_